00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:43.960
This looks fantastic, 
can you tell us a little bit about the hāngi?

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.240
Yeah, so the hāngi is obviously 

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.480
our traditional Māori way of cooking food or feasting.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:52.480
Ours is unique as far as I know because we actually 

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.800
set the hāngi in the ground 
and bury it over so it’s actually subterranean.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.320
‘hā’ is breath or also flavour, 

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:06.960
and ‘ngi ngi’ is a very old word for, to burn, 
to make smoky, to make fire.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:13.120
So that’s the origin of the word hāngi,
so the flavour that is smoky and has been burnt.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.120
So today when we came up,
the first thing you notice, inevitably,

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.000
is this beautiful island here, can you tell me a bit about it?

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.440
Yeah so that island is Moutohorā.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.320
The island is now pest free, 
it’s a protective wildlife reserve. 

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.440
Neil here is actually heavily involved.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:31.440
Yeah so there was a whole bunch of work.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.480
If you look at old pictures of Moutohorā, actually, 

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.400
it didn’t even have very many trees on it. 

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.280
The Department of Conservation and the Iwi, 
Ngāti Awa, worked really closely together. 

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.560
There was a whole bunch of trapping work 
that was done to make it pest free.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.680
A lot of our native birds and animals in New Zealand
evolved without mammalian predators, 

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:57.440
so Moutohorā, because it’s pest free, we have birds 
on there that are actually extinct on the mainland.

